From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Travis Gilbert <1travisgilbert@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 15:32:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 16/18] fix(input): choose the keymap at runtime, so the web
 gets the right one

Every keybinding in this repository was gated on
`#[cfg(target_os = "macos")]`, which is a question the web cannot
answer. One wasm binary is served to a Mac and to a Windows PC and
`target_os` is `unknown` for both, so every Mac user of a GPUI web
application received the Windows keymap: `cmd-a`, `cmd-c`, `cmd-v` and
`cmd-z` were bound to no action at all. Measured in Chrome with
`navigator.platform` reporting `MacIntel`: Meta+A appended to the
composer while Control+A selected.

Three shapes, three answers:

  - True mirrors -- Command here, Control there -- become `secondary-`,
    which is what that prefix has always meant. That covers copy in the
    two Root views and copy and select-all in TextView.

  - The Input keymap is not a mirror. macOS puts MoveHome on ctrl-a and
    word movement on alt-arrows; elsewhere ctrl-a is SelectAll and
    ctrl-arrows move by word. Those are two keymaps, not one keymap with
    a modifier swapped, so the whole 33/17 split is selected at runtime
    and appended to the 24 shared bindings. The inspector's
    cmd-alt-i/ctrl-shift-i is the same shape at smaller scale.

  - `Kbd::format` renders the keycap a person reads. Left alone it would
    print `Ctrl+A` above a shortcut that now answers to Command, which
    is worse than either vocabulary alone, so it asks the same question
    the keymap asked.

`gpui::secondary_modifier_is_platform` falls back to
`cfg!(target_os = "macos")` when nothing set it, so every native target
and every existing test keeps its current behavior.
